{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/109184-first-story-recap","id":109184,"title":"First Story Recap","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-09T04:51:28-07:00","id":109184,"title":"First Story Recap","content":"Not long ago I read the quickstart rules for PtC and instantly became hooked. It didn't take me long to track down all five books and quickly buy them. When I first picked it up, I had no idea it was all about alchemy. I just assumed it was a &quot;Frankenstein&quot; game. I majored in Chemistry and have always been a huge fan of science history.I have purchased other nWoD games, and while I enjoy them, I just keep coming back to Promethean.<br /> <br /> We just concluded the first story, called Florida '78, in our Chronicle I am running for a friend of mine. It began with his character waking up submerged in mud inside of a partially sunken camper (the kind that can attach to a pickup truck bed). Upon crawling out the broken front window, passed tatters of pages stapled to wood paneled walls, he emerged into a bizarre section of the Florida everglades that was strangely bone dry and dusty (save for the muddy paste inside the trailer).<br /> <br /> Eventually he found his way back to a shack at the heart of the Wasteland where he encounters his &quot;Papa&quot;, a golem named No One. We simulated the passage of time by playing what I called interludes over the next few weeks. I would send him in-character pages from No One's journal detailing these early days and end the entries with out-of-character chose your own adventure type choices. Depending on his choice, or rolls, he would receive small exp rewards or skill specializations.<br /> <br /> We picked up our next session several months later in game time, having had No One inform him of the basics of the Promethean condition and starting him along the path of Copper. He was eventually allowed to wander the Wasteland alone, but warned to stay away from the roads by his &quot;Papa&quot;. Naturally drawn to the sounds of the outside world, he one day breaks his father's ban while No One was off getting supplies. The newly Created witnesses a horrific car accident and tries to help the victims.<br /> <br /> The whole family is killed save for a severely wounded small girl. Despite the words of his father echoing in his head, the young Promethean follows signs to a nearby tourist trap called &quot;The Grinning Gator Diner and Reptile Farm&quot;. We ended the session as he began his journey towards it.<br /> <br /> A theme of the first few sessions was the fact that my player had not yet chosen a name. Little did he know that this was one of his milestones. We started the next session with a mid abuse flash back in which No One had lashed out due to frustration. In the violence he kept shouting &quot;Who are you?? Who are you?!?&quot; in hopes of catalyzing the players progress towards self. It was at this point the character said his name and achieved his first milestone.<br /> <br /> Flash back to the road. The character arrives at the diner only to find it abandoned. He finds an old pay phone booth and dials the operator despite extreme static on the line. As he speaks he hears his own voice in some feedback and realizes his blunt unnatural language is clearly inhuman. He succeeds on a resolve + socialize roll however and manages to communicate the details of the accident. As he hangs up the phone, something feels.....wrong....<br /> <br /> He decides to leave (and bypass the entire scene I designed at the diner, but thats ok), and notes how the garbage that litters the lot seems to form a strange pattern with the vulgar graffiti that is everywhere. The pattern swirls, and seems to coalesce on the chest of a large alligator stature at the lot's entrance. There it forms a mysterious mark that calls out to the character. Passing a wits + occult, he interprets it simply as &quot;Run!&quot;. His attention is drawn away as small curls of paint begin to fall like snow in front of the mark. Raising his eyes, he sees the paint at the top of the statue slowly peeling away as an impossibly thin figure crests its top.<br /> <br /> The Pandoran screams, activating Demon's Call, which causes the large stationary alligators sunning on the nearby road to slowly shift their massive bulks and begin dragging themselves towards the parking lot. The player runs and wins several contested rolls to get away from the Pandoran back into the swamps.....or so he thinks. Stopping after some time to take it all in, he is assaulted by the partially calcified Pandoran drawn to his unused Vitriol (won't make that mistake again).<br /> <br /> They begin a running battle (I came to realize Pandorans and Promtheans are REALLY durable) in which the creature continually shuts down the player's Natural Weaponry (big calloused/stone fists) using Inertia. It finally ended with the player pounding the creature to dust. Such was his frustration, and subsequent zeal of destruction, that he checked for Torment. As his, inhuman, warped fists rained blow after blow, the player blacked out.<br /> <br /> He awoke sometime later, sitting with his head hung low, amidst a Florida thunderstorm. As the rain poured down, it felt so different from dryness of No One's wasteland. This scene worked out great, asking questions that lead the player to resolve that his lot in life was to serve No One and recognizing the consequences of disobedience. Despondent, he returned to the shack......only to find it burnt to the ground.<br /> <br /> The remains were long ago cold, looking to have burnt several weeks ago. He had only been gone several hours though.....hadn't he? As he dug through the rubble, he removed some debris covering the hole in which No One slept to reveal (this is one of several times I let the player dictate plot. I wanted it to be our story, not mine) nothing. No One was no where to be found.<br /> <br /> The encounter with the Pandoran left the player feeling inhuman. It, and he, contrasted the warmth of the little girl he had tried to save. He had told himself he had a life, but he now realized it wasn't real. The pitiful existence he shared for months with No One was just a mockery. And now, even that was gone. With no foundation, and so many examples of what he was not, the player felt a void inside of him. It was at the moment of despair that he thought back to No One's words concerning the soul. Could that empty space be filled? Through the path of Copper, could he actually refine a soul from his impure substances as No One has hinted? Was he more than just a servant?<br /> <br /> This is where we ended the session, and concluded Florida '78.<br /> <br /> My player and I discussed where he would like to go next. We created the plot device of &quot;The Duffel Bag&quot;, that contains burnt scraps of No One's journals, odd momentos, religious text fragments, etc. In digging through it, the character will find clues that will give him a choice of where he would like to go next. I am going to advance time from 1978 to the early 80s and offer him three destinations to pick from. <br /> <br /> As I said before, I am loving this game. I have read the first 3 books cover to cover, and am currently working through the 4th. Thanks for reading!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2676-mramericium","name":"MrAmericium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-09T05:33:42-07:00","id":109205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrAmericium</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/109184-first-story-recap#post109184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They begin a running battle (I came to realize Pandorans and Promtheans are REALLY durable) in which the creature continually shuts down the player's Natural Weaponry (big calloused/stone fists) using Inertia. It finally ended with the player pounding the creature to dust. Such was his frustration, and subsequent zeal of destruction, that he checked for Torment. As his, inhuman, warped fists rained blow after blow, the player blacked out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds pretty awesome. Minor point: you say the player blacked out. I assume you mean the character? Not your friend?<br /> <br /> Is it just the two of you involved in the game?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-09T07:36:54-07:00","id":109281,"title":"","content":"Hah, sorry yes the character. I would have been pretty proud if it was so intense the player actually blacked out.<br /> <br /> It is just the two of us, which I think works really well for PtC. I'm not sure how such a personal, often perspective skewing, story would work with a group. I have some other friends who are interested in playing though, so given the roaming nature of the chronicle a drop in drop out scenario could work. I'm pretty sure that joining a throng is a milestone I'd like to explore.<br /> <br /> Have you run a PtC game before? If so, any tips?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2676-mramericium","name":"MrAmericium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-10T00:38:12-07:00","id":109938,"title":"","content":"I'm not a Promethean player, but that recap was a great read. The only question bugging me at the end was what happened to the injured girl? Did the Promethean leave her at the crash site when he went for help?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-10T18:44:28-07:00","id":110481,"title":"","content":"No, sorry, I haven't run a PtC game. I'd really like to, but could never get the group to give it a try","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-10T20:35:03-07:00","id":110548,"title":"","content":"I can now say I have 3 Promethean games under my belt, which is good enough I think.<br /> <br /> Tips for running Promethean? Don't be afraid to stomp on your players emotions. I had them save a little girl from an abusive mother (hostage situation) and they thought everything was fine and they did the good thing. Then the mother committed suicide due to her only child being removed from her, then they discovered that the girl was a Scion (a mortal immune to the disquiet) and their emotions were all across the board. My players were lost in their emotions, not my characters. It's hard to achieve, but it feels so good every time.<br /> <br /> Another tip which I fail to follow myself: Try to let your players feel that they can stay at a single place for some time without dying. The wasteland should build slowly, as for disquiet. Let them have a little job. Let them settle down and get some routine going. That's the best way for them to understand that they have the time to solve some problems around or work on a downtime project.<br /> <br /> The story should usually be around mortals. Supernaturals are fine but should not be the only thing going on. In other games (mages/vamp/werewolf) I sometimes feel like mortals have no meaning (other than to feed the vamps). Mages are nice because they have a SUPERSOUL instead of a normal one. Gheist have a second chance at REAL life. Changelings are slowly losing theirs, etc... but the true meaning of promethean should be normal mortals. Cultists willing to sell their soul for power is a classic one that gets the Promethean thinking about the true value of a soul, for example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Glorious Useless-Post Prana<br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Cost: 2</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">m; </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mins: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Linguistics 1, Essence 1 </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Type: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Simple</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Keywords: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mute</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Duration: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Instant</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Prerequisite Charms: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Ten-Characters Methodology</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","name":"Magnum Opus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2589_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"821"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-12T09:17:37-07:00","id":111532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/109184-first-story-recap?p=109938#post109938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only question bugging me at the end was what happened to the injured girl? Did the Promethean leave her at the crash site when he went for help?</div> </div> </div> </div>After succeeding on his rolls to communicate with the operator, he was assured that help had been dispatched. He didn't get much time to go back in check due to the Pandorans coming out of dormancy. After the attack, the character was too shaken to do anything other than run back to his Master (golems right?).<br /> <br /> @Magnum Opus<br /> Thank you for the advice, I've done my best thus far to &quot;stomp on&quot; the emotions. Did you find it difficult doing that for more than 1 player? On a related note, how did it run as a group game? Lastly, cool idea on the cultists.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2676-mramericium","name":"MrAmericium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-13T06:23:37-07:00","id":112027,"title":"","content":"I think that the important point in a group game of Promethean is to strike the common ground. Go for events or things in which anyone can be involved. Like I said earlier, my players saved a Scion, and thus wanted to protect her, for she was the only human in existence able to withstand them. They all started to act really differently around the girl, and that's the cool thing =)<br /> <br /> Another game I GMed was against a group of hunters (The Sons of Adam) who were Pyros addicted and acted like Pyros-vamps for the group. Wherever they went, they would follow, and they would be a threat. Thus the players had to work together often. But don't force them to always be together. If able, play solo or duo chapters once in a while, or forum plays, in order to keep the character-oriented story that is Promethean.<br /> <br /> - Magnum Opus","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Glorious Useless-Post Prana<br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Cost: 2</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">m; </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mins: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Linguistics 1, Essence 1 </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Type: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Simple</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Keywords: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mute</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Duration: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Instant</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Prerequisite Charms: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Ten-Characters Methodology</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","name":"Magnum Opus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2589_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"821"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-25T04:41:16-07:00","id":119032,"title":"","content":"Hello, I will love to read another recap from you ! It was a very good start.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2809-batro","name":"batro","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-26T14:27:59-07:00","id":119971,"title":"","content":"Thank you for sharing your game. You know I have tried several times to get a Promethean tabletop game going, but the players around my area (that I am aware of) aren't really into games like Promethean - their attentions are too short for a goal like becoming human.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2826-mikerand","name":"mikerand","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2826_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}